# Device identity & security

A compromised control path must not be enough to force an unverified model
onto the device. The agent is the last line of defence for **artifact verify**.

**Who holds device-root credentials** (agent process vs host supervisor vs
secure element provisioned elsewhere) is **product topology** — consumer ADR.
This file does not require the agent to own join secrets or the device-root
MQTT identity.

## Non-negotiable baseline (agent craft)

1. **Independent on-device signature verification** before activation — the
   agent can refuse an artifact even if the cloud "approved" it.
2. **Never log** private keys, cert PEMs, tokens, or join secrets — even
   partially, even at debug (**`logging-loguru.mdc`**).
3. When the agent **does** open MQTT (see **`mqtt-transport.mdc`**): prefer
   **mTLS**; no anonymous or password-only MQTT in production without an ADR
   with expiry; prefer **per-device topic ACLs** so one device cannot
   impersonate another.
4. **Trust anchors** used for manifest/artifact verify are provisioned, not
   learned over an untrusted first contact (avoid trust-on-first-use holes).
   Exact provisioning path is consumer ADR / ops.

## Secrets and logging

- Secret **values** are not constitution content; rotation and vault mechanics
  live in consumer ops / IAC.
- Bound identity handed to the agent by a host process (if any) is still
  secret material — same logging ban.

